menu "BYOK display driver"

    config BYOK_DISPLAY_TRUE_SYSTEM_RESET
        bool "Send a real UC1611 System Reset command before the vendor init preamble"
        default y
        help
            The vendor's own reset preamble sends 0xE1 to the panel's
            COMMAND address (0x38) followed by 0xE2 to its DATA address
            (0x39) -- a double-byte opcode whose meaning is UNKNOWN
            (hw_config.h Sec.3.1/13). It never sends 0xE2 as an actual
            command (CD=0), which on the UC1611/UC1611s command set is
            "Set System Reset", a genuine single-byte controller reset.

            The panel has no reset GPIO and is not power-cycled by an S3
            warm reset (docs/recovery.md §6, CONFIRMED
            from both the vendor disassembly and this driver's own code).
            On a cold boot that is invisible -- the controller's own
            power-on reset has already put it in a known state before the
            21-entry init table runs. On a warm reset (RESET S3, or any
            esp_restart()) the controller is NOT reset, so the init table
            is written over whatever state the previous run left: display
            RAM, the VBIAS/contrast register if changed at runtime, scroll
            position, the partial-window, the inverse-display flag, the
            charge-pump state -- a STRONGLY INDICATED mechanism for the
            "display artifacts, wrong contrast" symptom observed after
            RESET S3 (observed during bring-up, reproduced on stock too).

            ON (the default, byok-mod 0.1.2+): before the vendor's
            unchanged 0xE1/0xE2 pair, send 0xE2 to the COMMAND address (a
            real System Reset) followed by a >=150 ms settle delay. Strictly
            additive -- the vendor sequence after it is byte-identical to
            before this option existed, so this is trivially revertible.

            OFF: exact vendor/stock parity, byte-for-byte -- the original
            0.1.0/0.1.1 behaviour.

    config BYOK_DISPLAY_FORCE_SCROLL_LINE_ZERO
        bool "Explicitly zero the UC1611 Set Scroll Line register at bring-up"
        default y
        help
            docs/troubleshooting.md: the first on-device
            photograph of BYOK-Mod's own output (0.1.8) showed the whole
            resting-screen image displayed 15 rows too high -- the 1-px top border and the "BYOK LAB" line
            pushed off the top of the glass, and the 15 glass rows they
            vacate at the bottom showing never-written controller RAM, not
            wrapped content. The shift is not a multiple of 8 (not a
            page/window-program error) and the image is not mirrored
            within its 8-row pages (not a framebuffer bit-order error).

            An exhaustive encode-and-match scan of every `call8 sim_send`
            site in the vendor's 1.1.0 IROM (20 sites total, all
            accounted for) found no byte in 0x40-0x7F sent anywhere in the
            vendor image, ever -- not at init, not at runtime; the 21-entry
            DROM init table this driver replays verbatim contains none
            either. 0x40 | SL[3:0] / 0x50 | SL[7:4] is the UC1611 "Set
            Scroll Line" pair, the controller's only row-granular vertical
            display origin. Neither the vendor firmware nor byok-mod ever
            programs it, and nothing in bring-up resets the controller
            (no display reset GPIO -- BYOK_LCD_HAS_RESET_GPIO 0; the
            vendor's 0xE1/DATA 0xE2 preamble is a RAM write, not a System
            Reset; CONFIG_BYOK_DISPLAY_TRUE_SYSTEM_RESET=n) -- so both
            firmwares simply inherit whatever the register holds coming
            out of its own power-on reset, which on this unit measured 15.

            Cause graded STRONGLY INDICATED, not CONFIRMED: Set Scroll
            Line is the only row-granular vertical origin in the public
            UC1611 Rev-0.81 command map, but this board is a UC1611s (I2C
            variant, outside that revision) and three opcodes (0xC9, 0x95,
            0xE1) remain unplaced -- see docs/troubleshooting.md Sec.2.

            ON (the default, byok-mod 0.1.9+): at the end of
            lcd_hw_reset_and_init(), immediately after the vendor's
            21-entry init table, send CMD 0x50 (Set Scroll Line MSB) then
            CMD 0x40 (Set Scroll Line LSB), both to the command address
            (0x38), programming SL=0. MSB before LSB is deliberate -- see
            the comment at the call site in byok_display.c for why that
            order is safe under either a UC1611 two-register decode or a
            UC1610-style single 6-bit decode. Strictly additive -- after
            the vendor's own unchanged init table, same policy as 0.1.2's
            TRUE_SYSTEM_RESET addition -- so this is trivially revertible.

            OFF: exact vendor/stock parity, byte-for-byte -- the register
            is left at whatever value it holds coming out of reset, as in
            every release through 0.1.8.

    config BYOK_DISPLAY_BULK_WRITES
        bool "Use bulk I2C transfers for panel data writes"
        default y
        help
            The stock vendor driver sends every framebuffer byte as its own
            one-byte I2C transaction to the data address (0x39) --
            BYOK_LCD_I2C_BYTES_PER_XFER == 1 in firmware/common/hw_config.h,
            CONFIRMED from the disassembly, and reproduced faithfully by
            this driver's fallback path (see below). That is 2400
            transactions for one full-panel refresh.

            The UC1611 auto-increments its column address after each data
            byte written inside the programmed window (hw_config.h Sec.3.3),
            so a full page row -- or, when the window spans the full panel
            width, the ENTIRE window -- can instead be sent as one single
            i2c_master_transmit() call to the data address. This is a
            protocol-level optimisation on top of CONFIRMED controller
            behaviour, not a guess about undocumented hardware, but it has
            never been exercised on the real panel -- that measurement is
            still pending.

            ON (the default): byok_display uses the bulk path. Full-width
            windows (PARTIAL_REFRESH/FULL_REFRESH covering x=0..239) are
            sent in one transaction; narrower partial windows are sent one
            transaction per 8-row page (still far fewer than one per byte).

            OFF: byok_display reproduces the stock per-byte behaviour
            exactly (one i2c_master_transmit() per framebuffer byte) --
            keep this as the fallback if bulk transfers ever prove
            unreliable on the real controller/wiring.

            Commands (the reset preamble, the 21-entry init table, window-
            program addressing, contrast) are ALWAYS sent one byte per
            transaction regardless of this setting -- SAFETY.md's own
            evidence-preservation stance and hw_config.h's own comment
            ("replay verbatim") apply to the init sequence specifically,
            and command traffic is a tiny fraction of a refresh's total
            byte count anyway, so there is nothing to gain by batching it.

    config BYOK_DISPLAY_POLARITY_EXPERIMENT
        bool "Run the command/data address polarity A/B/C experiment at boot"
        default n
        help
            0.1.8: default flipped back to n. The vendor's sim_send()/
            sim_reset() were disassembled end-to-end (docs/display.md Sec.6) (the vendor functions this whole
            experiment was testing an alternative theory of) and found
            byok-mod's transport ALREADY vendor-exact -- one 1-byte I2C
            write per LCD byte to 0x38 (command) / 0x39 (data), no strobe,
            no control byte, no read path, exactly what this option's own
            "pass A"/unswapped mapping already does. The 0.1.7 on-device
            read-back that motivated this experiment (0xFF -> 0x00,
            identical from both addresses) turned out to be one electrical
            node (SDA), not two expander latches -- see that analysis's
            Sec.6. This option and its A/B/C runner are KEPT, not
            removed -- an independent on-device polarity check remains
            cheap and the finding above does not itself explain why the
            panel stays blank -- but it no longer runs by default now that
            its motivating hypothesis has a documented, instruction-level
            answer. Flip back to y to re-run it manually.

            docs/display.md Sec.6 re-derived the command(0x38)/data(0x39)
            address polarity from the vendor disassembly AND the real
            UltraChip UC1611 datasheet (16/19 init-table opcodes match
            exactly, C/D bit included) and found NO inversion -- the
            current mapping is CONFIRMED correct. This option exists
            regardless of that outcome, by deliberate choice, to also
            gather on-device evidence: with the panel
            still blank on real hardware despite a byte-for-byte,
            opcode-correct vendor-identical stream, an on-device A/B
            comparison costs little and is independent confirming (or,
            if it surprises us, falsifying) evidence for Sec.6's verdict.

            ON: byok_display_run_boot_selftest() is REPLACED, for this
            build only, by byok_display_run_polarity_experiment(), which
            runs three full reset+init+window-program passes in sequence,
            each preceded/followed by a UC1611 STATUS READ attempt
            (i2c_master_receive()) on both 0x38 and 0x39, logged either
            way:
              Pass A: current mapping, cmd=0x38 / data=0x39 (vendor
                      parity) -- 8px checkerboard held 3 s, logged
                      "POLARITY A: cmd=0x38 data=0x39".
              Pass B: SWAPPED mapping, cmd=0x39 / data=0x38 -- full
                      re-init under the swapped mapping, checkerboard
                      held 3 s, logged "POLARITY B: cmd=0x39 data=0x38".
              Pass C: whichever docs/display.md Sec.6 says is correct
                      (= pass A's mapping, per that section's verdict) --
                      full re-init, then a "POLARITY A OK?" banner left
                      up as the resting screen, same as the normal
                      self-test's resting state.

            OFF: normal byok_display_run_boot_selftest() (checkerboard,
            border+banner, line test, boot-original-gate screen, resting
            screen) runs as before -- this option's default in every
            release before 0.1.7 and after this experiment's evidence has
            been gathered.

            The command/data mapping is a runtime variable
            (byok_display_set_polarity()), not a rebuild -- both I2C
            device handles already exist at both addresses; only which
            handle byok_display's internal lcd_send()/lcd_send_bulk_data()
            treat as "command" vs "data" changes.

endmenu
